---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
const idx = [
  ["Zampiello", 439, 259, 104, 16, 19, 10, 0],
  ["Annecchino", 603, 63, 215, 237, 40, 0, 9],
];
const avail = [
  ["Arpaia", 99, 61, 61, 43, "Zampiello home town"],
  ["Forchia", 110, 71, 73, 73, "Annecchino home town"],
  ["Moiano", 131, 82, 109, 99, "second cluster of both — unexamined"],
  ["Paolisi", 100, 70, 63, 43, "where Francesco Falco came from in 1714 — unexamined"],
];
---
<Base title="Antenati">
  <h1>What Antenati holds</h1>
  <p class="lede">The Italian State Archives' own portal, which for most of this research was closed to
  us. Opened by hand, it answers two questions that had been left hanging — and the answers are not the
  ones we wanted.</p>

  <h2>Two missing volumes</h2>
  <p>Two people in this archive could not be documented: <strong>Angelo Zampiello</strong>, born at
  Arpaia on 31 August 1895, and <strong>Filomena Annecchino</strong>, born at Forchia on 10 April 1897.
  Neither appears in FamilySearch, and neither appears in Antenati's name index.</p>
  <p>The reason is now clear, and it is the same in both cases. <strong>The volume is not
  digitised.</strong> The archive's own shelf-marks give it away — they run consecutively and skip a
  year:</p>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Arpaia, births</h3>
      <p class="mono small">1890·591 · 1891·592 · 1892·593 · 1893·594 · 1894·595 —
      <strong style="color:var(--accent)">1895 absent</strong> —
      1896·596 · 1897·597 · 1898·598 · 1899·599 · 1900·600</p>
      <p class="small">The shelf-marks do not skip. The year does.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Forchia, births</h3>
      <p class="mono small">1890·3654 · 1891·3655 · 1892·3656 · 1893·3657 · 1894·3658 · 1895·3659 ·
      1896·3660 — <strong style="color:var(--accent)">1897 absent</strong> —
      1898·3661 · 1899·3662 · 1900·3663</p>
      <p class="small">The same pattern, two years and one province apart.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>So these are not research failures. They are <strong>two specific volumes</strong>, each holding one
  of the two births this archive cannot prove, and each sitting un-scanned in the
  <strong>Archivio di Stato di Benevento</strong>. Anyone able to order or read them would close both
  questions at once.</p>

  <h2>Moiano — a cluster nobody had looked at</h2>
  <p>Antenati's name index was harvested in full for both surnames: <strong>439 Zampiello</strong> and
  <strong>603 Annecchino</strong> records. They do not sort the way the FamilySearch harvest suggested.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Surname</th><th>Total</th><th>Arpaia</th><th>Moiano</th><th>Forchia</th><th>S. Agata</th><th>Airola</th><th>Dugenta</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{idx.map(([n,t,a,m,f,s,ai,d]) => (
      <tr><td><strong>{n}</strong></td><td class="mono">{t}</td><td class="mono">{a}</td>
        <td class="mono" style="background:var(--bg)"><strong>{m}</strong></td>
        <td class="mono">{f}</td><td class="mono">{s}</td><td class="mono">{ai}</td><td class="mono">{d}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p><strong>Moiano is the second stronghold of both families</strong> — 104 Zampiello and 215
  Annecchino — and until now this archive had never once looked there. It sits in the same valley, about
  ten kilometres from Arpaia. Whether the Moiano Zampiello are the same kindred as the Arpaia Zampiello
  is untested; with 37 bearers left in Italy it would be surprising if they were not.</p>

  <h2>The seam that has not been touched</h2>
  <p>Beyond the name index, Antenati holds the register images themselves — and among them the
  <em>processetti</em>, the marriage supplement files. These are the richest genealogical source in the
  Italian system: attached to each marriage are the couple's birth extracts, the parents' consents, and
  the death certificates of any parent already dead. One file can carry two generations backwards.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Town</th><th>Births</th><th>Marriages</th><th>Deaths</th><th>Processetti</th><th></th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{avail.map(([t,n,m,d,p,w]) => (
      <tr><td><strong>{t}</strong></td><td class="mono">{n}</td><td class="mono">{m}</td>
        <td class="mono">{d}</td><td class="mono"><strong>{p}</strong></td><td class="small">{w}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p><strong>258 volumes of marriage supplements</strong> across these four towns, none of them read.
  That is where Matteo Falco's parentage would be found, if it survives anywhere.</p>


  <h2>Moiano and Paolisi, swept</h2>
  <p>The name index was harvested town by town — 1,087 records.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Town</th><th>Falco</th><th>Zampiello</th><th>Annecchino</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Paolisi</strong></td><td class="mono"><strong>406</strong></td><td class="mono">0</td><td class="mono">1</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Moiano</strong></td><td class="mono"><strong>310</strong></td><td class="mono">104</td><td class="mono">215</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Forchia</td><td class="mono">31</td><td class="mono">16</td><td class="mono">237</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Arpaia</td><td class="mono">22</td><td class="mono">259</td><td class="mono">63</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Arienzo</td><td class="mono">0 — not indexed</td><td class="mono">—</td><td class="mono">—</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p><strong>Paolisi holds the largest Falco cluster in the valley</strong> — and it is where Francesco
  Falco came from when he turned up at Arienzo in 1714.</p>

  <h3>And a caution that matters more than the counts</h3>
  <p>716 Falco records at Paolisi and Moiano is a great many, and the temptation is to absorb them. The
  in-law names say don't. The Arienzo Falco marry <strong>Crisci</strong> (113 register pages),
  <strong>Morgillo</strong> (76), <strong>Martone</strong> (49), <strong>de Lucia</strong> (40). Set
  that against the others:</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Town</th><th>Who the Falco there married</th><th>Shared with Arienzo</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Paolisi</strong></td><td class="small">Carbone, Maietta, Lanni, De Gregorio, Nola, Maddaloni, Perrotta, Guerriero, Mauro, Girardi, Tirone, Cioffi</td><td><strong>Ruggiero, Rivetti</strong> only</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Moiano</strong></td><td class="small">Amoriello, Maglione, Tedesco, Massaro, Cesare, Tirino, Buonanno, Palma, Mauriello, Izzo, Ciaramella, Iodice</td><td><strong>Morgillo</strong> only</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>These look like <strong>largely different populations</strong> — which is exactly what this
  archive's founding premise predicts, since <em>Falco</em> was coined independently many times over.
  <span class="chip">caveat</span> the comparison sets 1861–1942 civil records against 1650–1830 parish
  records, so some divergence follows from the dates alone. Suggestive, not conclusive — and not a
  reason to merge anyone into the line.</p>
  <p class="small">One further negative worth recording: across all 1,042 indexed Zampiello and
  Annecchino records there is <strong>no Zampiello–Annecchino marriage</strong>. They shared four towns
  for a century without marrying each other.</p>

  <h2>The processetti — and one volume that matters</h2>
  <p><strong>321 volumes</strong> of marriage supplements: Arienzo 63, Forchia 73, Arpaia 43,
  Moiano 99, Paolisi 43. Three hard constraints came out of the reconnaissance.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>They stop at 1865.</strong> Forchia and Arpaia return nothing for 1866–1950 — so the
    marriages of Carminantonio and Filomena in the 1920s, and of Angelo Zampiello and Giuseppa Ferrara,
    are not there. Nor are the marriage registers for those years.</li>
    <li><strong>Arienzo is not name-indexed at all</strong> — zero records for Falco or Crisci. The
    home parish can only be read as images.</li>
    <li><strong>The images are Cloudflare-blocked to direct fetch.</strong> The IIIF manifest is open
    and gives every page URL, but those URLs return a block; they render only inside the viewer on the
    record page.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>The target is identified.</strong> Vincenzo Falco married Andreana Crisci about 1817, and
  the Arienzo processetti for <strong>1817</strong> survive — ark <span class="mono">an_ua14158</span>,
  shelf-mark 66.1021, <strong>310 pages</strong>. Under Neapolitan practice a marriage file attaches the
  groom's birth extract, the parents' consents, and <strong>the death certificates of any parent or
  paternal grandparent already dead</strong>.</p>
  <p>If Matteo Falco's death act survives anywhere, that volume is where it will be — and it would name
  <strong>Matteo's own father</strong>, which is the question this archive has never been able to
  answer. Reading it means paging a viewer through 310 images. Slow, but it is the obvious next piece
  of work.</p>

  <h2>What this changes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Antenati is <strong>reachable</strong>. Earlier work recorded it as blocked; it is not, and the
    <a href={u("/open-questions")}>open questions</a> have been updated accordingly.</li>
    <li>Two unproven births are now <strong>located rather than merely missing</strong> — a specific
    volume each, in a specific archive.</li>
    <li><strong>Moiano and Paolisi</strong> join the list of places this family has to be searched in.</li>
    <li>The <em>processetti</em> are the largest unworked seam — 321 volumes — but they stop at 1865,
    which rules them out for the twentieth-century marriages.</li>
    <li><strong>Arienzo processetti 1817</strong> is the single most promising unread document in the
    project.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
